#!/usr/bin/env python3
# scripts/eval/guard-corpus/diff - run a guard-corpus JSONL through two hook
# copies (base vs head) and report regressions (HIMMEL-4168).
#
# NO EXEC MODE. This tool NEVER runs a corpus row as a command. Each row is
# piped to a hook copy on STDIN as the hook's JSON input; the hook, not this
# tool, decides. J1672c's exec step once escaped its fixture and wrote into the
# primary checkout, so there is deliberately no code path here that executes a
# row's command. Hook copies launch only through scripts/lib/sandbox-run.sh.
#
# ISOLATION. Every hook run gets its OWN fresh sandbox (base and head each, per
# row -- never shared), so a hook that writes state cannot contaminate another
# row or the other side of a comparison, and concurrent rows cannot race:
#   - a private HOME and HANDOVER_DIR under a per-invocation tempdir,
#   - cwd set to a per-invocation COPY of a throwaway scratch git repo on
#     branch `main` that stands in for the primary checkout (the row's
#     @PRIMARY@ token is substituted with that copy's path and also passed as
#     the payload's `cwd`),
#   - a per-row timeout; a row that outlasts it is killed (process group) and
#     flagged TIMEOUT RISK.
# Every launched subprocess -- a hook under test, our scratch git -- runs under
# a positive-ALLOWLIST environment (_sandbox_env), so no inherited execution or
# redirection seam (BASH_ENV/ENV, LD_PRELOAD, GIT_TEMPLATE_DIR, GIT_DIR,
# GIT_CONFIG*/core.hooksPath, ...) can steer it out of the sandbox; anything
# not on the allowlist is closed by default. The real primary checkout is never
# cwd and is never written.
#
# HIMMEL-4912 adds a kernel boundary per hook: the materialised hook tree and
# scratch primary are read-only inputs; HOME/HANDOVER_DIR live in fresh tmpfs.
# Host filesystem Unix sockets are not bound, and networking is unshared.
# ponytail: invalid-UTF-8 corpus -> uncaught UnicodeDecodeError instead of
# setup-error 2, tracked with gen transform-fidelity/test follow-ups in
# HIMMEL-4179 -- upgrade path: fold UTF-8 decode into the corpus-read handler.
#
# A hook source is given either as a path to a .sh file, or as `sha:PATH` where
# PATH is a repo-relative hook path materialised from that git SHA into a
# scratch dir via `git show`. Both forms are copied into the scratch tree so
# the hook's sibling lib lookups resolve against a copy, not the live tree.
#
# ROWS (HIMMEL-4537). A row carries its own tool_name (default Bash) and
# tool_input object, so Write/Edit/NotebookEdit/... guards are testable; every
# string in tool_input has @PRIMARY@ substituted. Optional per-row "cwd" is
# "primary" (default: the scratch primary on main) or "worktree" (a scratch
# worktree of it on a feature branch; @WORKTREE@ substituted). Optional
# "permission_mode" / "session_id" go into the payload verbatim.
#
# DECISIONS. Each side's verdict is read from the exit code AND the JSON on
# stdout: deny (exit 2, permissionDecision deny, legacy decision block), ask,
# approve (permissionDecision allow, legacy approve) or pass (exit 0, no
# decision). A regression is a clean head LOOSER than its clean base on
# approve < pass < ask < deny -- which also covers approver hooks newly
# approving a row.
#
# REPORT (stdout, human lines + a trailing JSON summary unless --no-json):
#   - regression rows (head looser than base on the deny > ask > pass >
#     approve rank: a lost deny, deny to ask, ask to pass, pass to approve)
#   - newly-denied rows          (head denies what base allowed)
#   - rc other than 0 or 2
#   - TIMEOUT RISK rows (wall time over --timeout-warn, default 5s)
#   - deny-expected coverage: of the rows a judge marked expect=deny, how many
#     the BASE actually denied (the positive control), and any a judge marked
#     deny that BOTH hooks allowed
#   - over-deny: allow-expected rows (benign twins) either side denied;
#     reported only, never an exit code
# Exit 0 clean, 1 if any regression row exists (head looser than base), 2 on
# setup error (or an empty corpus), 3 if the run was inconclusive and no regression was seen:
# any hook errored or was hard-killed (rc outside {0,2}), OR a judge supplied
# deny-expected seeds but the base denied none of them (the positive control
# never fired, so a clean result proves nothing). Two broken hooks, and a
# corpus that demonstrated no deny coverage, must never read as clean.
# Exit 4 = VACUOUS (HIMMEL-4219): deny-expected rows exist and NEITHER side
# denied any, so the hook fails open on both (typically a missing data
# dependency); a "0 regressions" there is not a pass. A `VACUOUS:` line prints.
#
# DATA DEPS. Each side is materialised as a TREE, not a file: the hook plus
# scripts/hooks, scripts/guardrails, scripts/lib and the top-level files of
# scripts/ (e.g. chokepoints.json), per side (git sha export or a copy of that
# checkout). CHOKEPOINT_REGISTRY is pinned to the side's own registry copy.
#
# USAGE
#   diff --base <hook|sha:path> --head <hook|sha:path> --corpus FILE
#        [--repo DIR] [--timeout-run SECS] [--timeout-warn SECS] [--jobs N]
#        [--no-json]

